1a. WHY THE OLD NOTES LIED  --  READ THIS
------------------------------------------
An earlier version of this file claimed Phases 2 and 4 were finished. They were
not. `lib/` really had been finished, but the TEST SIDE OF PHASE 2 HAD NEVER
STARTED: the three Scan test files could not compile.

`flutter analyze` was reporting 114 issues, not the number recorded above, and
roughly 100 of them came out of a single typo:

    test/scan_screen_test.dart:2
    test/mlkit_text_recognizer_test.dart:4
        import 'package:flutter/test/flutter_test.dart';

That library has never existed. It is `package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart`.
One wrong import cascaded into ~77 phantom `testWidgets` / `find` / `expect`
"isn't defined" errors and made the entire test tree look broken.

Three further traps, each of which read as a code defect but was a stale test:

  - `test/scan_screen_test.dart` imported `widgets/scan_button.dart` and
    `widgets/scan_button_bar.dart`. NEITHER FILE EXISTS -- the `widgets/`
    directory under the scan feature is empty. There is no `ScanButton` class
    anywhere in `lib/`. The real control is a `FilledButton.icon` with
    `Key('scanButton')`.
  - It asserted `find.text('Line 1')` against a screen that renders
    `Line 1: "Amox 500mg"`, and looked for a `'Suggestion '` label that exists
    nowhere in the codebase.
  - `test/mlkit_text_recognizer_test.dart` called the PRIVATE
    `recognizer._checkUsable()` from another library. Private members are
    library-scoped; that cannot compile under any fix to the import.

Lesson for whoever picks this up: a checkbox is only evidence if the file
compiles and its tests run. "Marked done" had been assumed from the plan.


